8. Epigenetic and Admixture Mapping Contributions
Beyond classical sequence-level variations, clinical severity and exacerbation patterns are influenced by epigenetic and ancestral elements:
DNA Methylation (DNAm): Epigenome-wide studies in Europeans have identified significant blood and cross-tissue nasal epithelial DNAm markers, notably cg25345365 annotated to ZBTB16 and markers annotated to BAIAP2, which are strongly associated with asthma exacerbations
 \cite{MartinGonzalezPerezGarcia2025}.
Ancestry-Specific Risk: Admixture mapping in Hispanic/Latino populations reveals that Indigenous American local ancestry is genome-wide significantly associated with increased odds of severe AEs at chromosome 5q32 in Mexican Americans (specifically rs10477350, OR: 1.73), whereas European ancestry is protective
 \cite{DharmagePerret2019}
.
