To the Editorial Board of the Journal of Asthma:
Understanding the biological heterogeneity of bronchial asthma requires a critical appraisal of how genetic predispositions vary across different age-at-onset populations
 \cite{ChenZheng2025}
. Epidemiological and clinical evidence has long established that childhood-onset asthma and adult-onset (late-onset) asthma represent distinct clinical phenotypes with divergent sex ratios, environmental triggers, atopic comorbidities, and severity profiles
 \cite{PividoriSchoettler2018}
. Recent large-scale genome-wide and transcriptome-wide association studies confirm that these differences are underpinned by partly distinct genetic architectures
 \cite{FawcettHall2025}
.
This systematic review synthesizes the current evidence comparing the strength, heritability, specific risk loci, and functional tissue profiles of genetic variants between pediatric and adult asthma populations.
1. Discrepancies in Global and SNP-Based Heritability (h 
2
 )
Family-based and twin studies demonstrate that childhood-onset asthma has a significantly stronger genetic component than adult-onset disease
 \cite{PividoriSchoettler2018}
:
Twin Heritability Estimates: While the heritability of asthma in adults is estimated to range between 35\\\\% and 74\\\\%
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
, heritability estimates in children and young adolescents consistently reach 80\\\\% to 90\\\\%
 \cite{CameronChristieMackay2020}
.
SNP-Based Liability Variance (h 
2
 ): Utilizing Linkage Disequilibrium Score regression (LDSC) on large-scale population data from the UK Biobank (UKB), researchers estimated the SNP-based heritability of childhood-onset asthma (onset before age 12) to be 0.33, which is more than three times larger than that of adult-onset asthma (onset between ages 26 and 65), estimated at 0.098
 \cite{PividoriSchoettler2018}
.
Explained Variance: Genome-wide significant SNPs account for 11\\\\% of the phenotypic variance in childhood-onset asthma, but only 1.6\\\\% of the variance in adult-onset asthma
 \cite{PividoriSchoettler2018}
 \cite{LaroccaMoreno2013}.
These quantitative genetics metrics indicate a predominant role for inherited genetic variation in the initiation of pediatric disease, whereas non-genetic, lifestyle, and environmental risk factors (such as active smoking, obesity, and occupational exposures) play a far greater role in the etiology of adult-onset asthma
 \cite{PividoriSchoettler2018}
.
