Systematic Review of Candidate Gene Polymorphisms and Asthma Exacerbations: Genetic Architecture, Clinical Severity, and Biomarker Utility
Authors: Generated by NotebookLM
Affiliation: Faculty of Medical Sciences, Department of Allergy and Pulmonology
Journal Target: Journal of Asthma
Abstract
Asthma exacerbations (AEs) represent the primary driver of asthma morbidity, mortality, and healthcare-associated costs. While early genetic research concentrated heavily on general susceptibility to disease onset, a paradigm shift has demonstrated that the genetic modifiers of clinical severity, pharmacogenomic response, and exacerbation frequency are distinct from those of general disease susceptibility. This systematic review synthesizes the current clinical and molecular evidence linking single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) across thirteen key candidate genes—ADRB2, IL4, IL4RA, TNFA, NR3C1, PPARG, TGFB1, IL10, CHI3L1, GSDMB, CTNNA3, ADIPOQ, and SEMA3D—to asthma exacerbation outcomes. We evaluate their impact on severe clinical endpoints, map underlying biological mechanisms, and analyze the moderating effects of gene-gene and gene-environment interactions. Furthermore, we address study-to-study heterogeneity driven by methodological differences and appraise the current clinical utility of these variants as predictive biomarkers for personalized medicine.
1. Clinical Severity Endpoints and Metrics (Question 7)
A primary challenge in translating genetic association studies to clinical practice is the standardization of outcomes. According to international consensus guidelines (ATS/ERS), severe asthma exacerbations (SAEs) are characterized by acute worsening of symptoms requiring systemic (oral or parenteral) corticosteroid bursts, emergency department (ED) visits, or hospitalizations to prevent fatal outcomes
 \cite{KimChang2022}
.
The candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) reviewed in this paper show distinct associations with these specific severity endpoints:
Systemic Corticosteroid Requirements: Multiple variants demonstrate a robust association with the frequency of oral corticosteroid (OCS) bursts. Intronic variants in GSDMB (rs7216389)
 \cite{LiChristenson2021}
, CMTR1 (rs2395672)
 \cite{DahlinDenny2015}
, CTNNA3 (rs7915695)
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
, and CRHR1 (rs242941)
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
 consistently correlate with increased OCS requirements despite guideline-conforming controller therapy.
Emergency Department (ED) Visits and Hospitalizations: Loci such as CDHR3 (rs6967330) are highly validated predictors of recurrent early-childhood wheezing and asthma hospitalizations
 \cite{YanForno2021}
. The HLA-DQB1 locus (rs56151658) has been identified through large-scale Biobank studies as a robust genetic driver of adult asthma hospitalizations (OR = 1.36)
 \cite{SonPark2022}
. Additionally, variants in IL1RL1 (rs13431828) correlate significantly with acute pediatric ED presentation and hospitalization rates
 \cite{DijkVijverberg2019}
.
Intensive Care Unit (ICU) Admissions and Intubation: The non-synonymous coding polymorphisms rs1805011 (E375A) and rs1801275 (Q576R or Q551R/Q576R) in the interleukin-4 receptor alpha (IL4RA) gene are uniquely associated with life-threatening, near-fatal exacerbation events requiring endotracheal intubation or mechanical ventilation in ICU settings
 \cite{SonPark2022}
.
