Thoughts
Methodological Sources of Heterogeneity in Genomic Studies of Asthma Exacerbations: A Systematic Review and Critical Appraisal
In the era of precision medicine, identifying robust genetic predictors of severe asthma exacerbations (SAEs) is paramount to optimizing therapeutic regimens and developing targeted clinical interventions
 \cite{XuLi2017}
 \cite{ShiZhang2022}. However, a major barrier to the clinical translation of these findings is the high degree of inconsistency and failure to replicate reported genetic associations across independent cohorts
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
. Unlike general asthma susceptibility, which has been extensively mapped in large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS)
 \cite{XuLi2017}
, the genetic architecture of asthma exacerbations is highly sensitive to study design and execution.
This systematic review evaluates the principal methodological and clinical factors contributing to study-to-study heterogeneity in the pharmacogenomics and genetics of asthma exacerbations.
1. Heterogeneity in Phenotypic Definitions and Outcome Metrics
One of the most profound sources of clinical and statistical heterogeneity is the lack of standardized, harmonized definitions for both the exacerbation phenotype and the corresponding control groups
 \cite{KarimiLahousse2019}
.
A. Imprecision in Exacerbation Definitions
Studies frequently combine clinically distinct acute events into a single, heterogeneous outcome
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}. Severe exacerbations are conventionally defined by the American Thoracic Society and European Respiratory Society (ATS/ERS) as acute worsening requiring systemic corticosteroids, emergency department (ED) visits, or hospitalizations
 \cite{ParkTantisira2017}
. However, significant differences in cohort definitions introduce substantial noise:
Composite vs. Restricted Outcomes: Some studies use highly restrictive endpoints, such as asthma-related hospitalizations
 \cite{YanForno2021}
, while others rely on composite outcomes that encompass oral corticosteroid (OCS) bursts, emergency care, general practitioner (GP) visits, or school/work absences
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
.
Definition Mismatch between Discovery and Replication: Large biobanks (e.g., the UK Biobank) often lack detailed primary care prescription data, forcing investigators to define exacerbations solely by hospitalization ICD-codes
 \cite{YanForno2021}. Attempting to replicate these biobank findings in pediatric or trial-based cohorts that define exacerbations primarily via OCS burst history regularly fails due to the underlying severity mismatch
 \cite{YanForno2021}
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}.
Varying Observation Windows: The time frame evaluated for the occurrence of an exacerbation ranges from 6 months, 12 months, 24 months, to "ever" in the patient's history, significantly altering the baseline probability of the event and introducing temporal bias
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
.
B. Selection and Definition of Control Populations
The genetic effect size can be artificially inflated or diluted depending on the definition of the comparison cohort:
Asthmatic Non-Exacerbators vs. Healthy Controls: True genetic predictors of exacerbation propensity must be identified by comparing asthmatic patients who experience exacerbations against asthmatic patients who remain exacerbation-free (non-exacerbators) under similar clinical conditions
 \cite{ParkTantisira2017}
. However, many large-scale databases use unselected, non-asthmatic healthy individuals as controls
 \cite{HerreraLuisLorenzoDiaz2021}
. Comparing exacerbators directly to healthy controls fails to differentiate between variants driving general asthma susceptibility and those specifically driving acute exacerbation severity
 \cite{HerreraLuisLorenzoDiaz2021}
 \cite{HerreraLuisEspuelaOrtiz2020}.
C. Retrospective Recall Bias vs. Prospective Standardized Tracking
Retrospective Questionnaires: Observational cohorts often rely on retrospective, self-reported questionnaires to capture historical exacerbation events over the preceding year, which are highly prone to recall bias and phenotypic imprecision
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
.
Prospective Trial Tracking: Conversely, clinical trial cohorts (such as CAMP or CARE) track exacerbation events prospectively using highly standardized treatment algorithms, minimizing misclassification but yielding smaller sample sizes
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
. This prospective-retrospective design discordance is a major driver of replication failure, as seen in the CTNNA3 locus
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
.
2. Cohort Design, Recruitment, and Clinical Settings
The environmental and clinical settings under which patients are recruited profoundly alter the statistical penetrance of risk genotypes.
