A. Controlled Clinical Trials vs. Real-World Biobanks
The failure to replicate the CTNNA3 (alpha-T-catenin) locus represents a classic example of cohort design heterogeneity
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}. The initial suggestive association was identified within highly standardized pediatric clinical trials (CAMP and CARE), which featured close care-provider attention, strict adherence monitoring, and standardized medication steps
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}.
In contrast, replication was sought in BioVU, a real-world Electronic Health Record (EHR) linked biobank
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}. Biobanks capture "snapshots" of active healthcare utilization (e.g., unscheduled emergency visits or OCS prescription refills) under highly heterogeneous, uncontrolled environments where treatment compliance, caregiver technique, and disease severity are highly variable
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
.
B. Age-at-Onset and Developmental Heterogeneity
Pediatric vs. Adult Populations: The genetic heritability of asthma differs dramatically by age, estimated at 80–90\\\\% in children compared to 35–74\\\\% in adults
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
.
Divergent Pathophysiology: Pediatric exacerbations are overwhelmingly triggered by ciliated physical barrier defects and viral pathogens (e.g., human rhinovirus C receptor variants in CDHR3)
 \cite{YanForno2021}
 \cite{PuranikForno2017}. Adult late-onset disease is less heritable, more lung-centered, and driven by chronic tissue remodeling, smoking, and metabolic comorbidities
 \cite{BaekJung2022}
. Extrapolating pediatric risk variants (e.g., GSDMB/ORMDL3) to adult cohorts often yields completely null results
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}.
3. Confounding by Treatment Regimen and Medication Adherence
Because many investigated loci represent pharmacogenetic modifiers of drug response, failing to control for background therapy and treatment adherence severely confounds statistical models.
Confounding by Drug Exposure: In investigating pharmacogenetic variants (such as ADRB2 rs1042713 or CRHR1 rs242941), the genetic effect is entirely contingent upon drug exposure
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}. In the PiCA consortium, the ADRB2 Arg16 risk allele was associated with a 1.52-fold increased risk of exacerbations strictly among children receiving combined ICS plus LABA, but not among those treated with ICS monotherapy or leukotriene receptor antagonists (LTRAs)
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}. Studies that fail to stratify cohorts by precise maintenance medications will inevitably mask these treatment-specific pharmacogenetic effects
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}
.
The Adherence Masking Effect: Real-world observational cohorts and pharmacy database registries can accurately track prescribed and dispensed medications, but they cannot measure actual patient adherence or inhaler technique
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
. Poor treatment compliance is a major independent driver of severe exacerbations
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
 \cite{SoodConnolly2018}; if a patient carrying a protective genotype is non-adherent, they may still experience frequent exacerbations, thereby obscuring the true genetic effect size
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
.
Confounding by Indication: In observational studies, patients with inherently more severe, exacerbation-prone asthma are preferentially prescribed higher treatment steps (e.g., combination LABA + high-dose ICS)
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
. This "confounding by indication" can lead to an underestimation of pharmacogenetic risk if disease severity is not rigorously controlled using clinical proxies such as the British Thoracic Society (BTS) or GINA treatment steps
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
.
4. Population Admixture, Linkage Disequilibrium, and Ancestral Divergence
The global transferability of candidate gene markers is limited by differences in the genetic backgrounds of diverse study populations
 \cite{ChenZheng2025}
 \cite{AslamVijverberg2024}.
Variations in Minor Allele Frequencies (MAFs): The frequency of risk alleles varies widely across ethnic groups
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
. For instance, the ADRB2 Gly16Arg allele frequency is significantly higher in Latino populations compared to Northern European cohorts
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}. Low-frequency or rare variants may be highly penetrant in specific founder or minority populations but virtually absent (monomorphic) in European-descent cohorts, leading to a complete failure of validation
 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}
 \cite{ChaChoi2023}.
Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) Decay: Standard genotyping arrays rely on common "tag" SNPs to represent larger haplotype blocks
 \cite{NevilleChoi2018}
. However, LD patterns decay much more rapidly in older, African-admixed populations compared to European populations, meaning that a European tag SNP will often fail to correlate with the true causal variant in African Americans or Latinos
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
.
Population Stratification: Admixed populations (such as Latinos or African Americans) possess a mosaic of ancestral chromosomal segments
 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}
 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}. If studies fail to rigorously adjust for genetic ancestry using ancestry-based principal components (PCs) or local ancestry inference, ancestral differences in baseline exacerbation rates can generate false-positive or false-negative associations
 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}
.
5. Statistical and Genotyping Methodology Discrepancies
The choice of statistical models, genetic models, and genotyping platforms dictates the power and reproducibility of genetic association studies
 \cite{ZhorinaAbramovskikh2019}
 \cite{PerezGarciaHernndezPrez2020}.
Candidate Gene Studies vs. Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS): Candidate-gene association studies are historically limited by a narrow, hypothesis-driven focus on hand-picked variants, often suffering from small sample sizes (<1000 subjects), selection bias, and a lack of correction for multiple testing
 \cite{ZhangHuang2022}
. GWAS and next-generation sequencing (NGS/exome sequencing) offer hypothesis-free, high-density genomic coverage but require massive sample sizes to overcome the stringent genome-wide significance threshold (P≤5×10 
−8
 )
 \cite{ShrinePortelli2019}
.
Genetic Model Misspecification: The vast majority of meta-analyses and primary association models assume a linear, additive genetic model (assessing risk per copy of the minor allele)
 \cite{TurnerFrancis2016}
. However, some causal variants operate via recessive, dominant, or co-dominant pathways
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
 \cite{FuFu2018}. Misspecifying the genetic model significantly reduces statistical power and can lead to discordant findings
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
 \cite{FuFu2018}.
Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium (HWE) Deviations: Selection bias, genotyping errors, or unrecognized population stratification can cause candidate variants to deviate significantly from HWE in the control population
 \cite{WuLarkin2015}
 \cite{XuLi2017}. Studies that fail to filter out SNPs in HWE disequilibrium risk reporting biased and non-reproducible association estimates
 \cite{WuLarkin2015}
 \cite{XuLi2017}.
Omission of Covariates and Confounders: Many meta-analyses are forced to pool unadjusted data because primary papers fail to report or adjust for critical clinical confounders—including sex, age, body mass index (BMI), active smoking status, atopy, and environmental exposures (e.g., passive smoke, air pollution, or domestic allergens)
 \cite{EdrisRoos2021}
