 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}
.
Future Strategy: Future studies must prioritize large-scale, multi-ethnic cohorts and apply whole-genome admixture mapping and ancestry-specific genomic risk models (such as polygenic risk scores optimized for diverse populations) to untangle the ancestral modifiers of clinical severity
 \cite{GrossmanOrtega2019}
.
2. Phenotypic Harmonization and Outbox Metric Standardization
The lack of standardized, clinical-grade phenotypic definitions across cohorts remains a major contributor to inter-study heterogeneity and replication failure
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
:
Outcome Definition Discrepancies: Studies frequently combine disparate acute events (composite outcomes including oral corticosteroid [OCS] bursts, emergency care, and school absences)
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
 or rely solely on retrospective self-reports, which introduce substantial recall bias and misclassification
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
. Conversely, large-scale biobanks often define exacerbations purely via retrospective ICD-coded hospitalization data, which miss milder, outpatient-managed flares
 \cite{YanForno2021}
.
Mismatched Control Groups: Many studies evaluate exacerbation risk by comparing cases with severe exacerbations directly to healthy, non-asthmatic controls
 \cite{YanForno2021}
. This design makes it virtually impossible to separate genetic variants driving general asthma susceptibility from those specifically modifying acute exacerbation propensity
 \cite{YanForno2021}
 \cite{HerreraLuisLorenzoDiaz2021}.
Future Strategy: Future protocols must systematically adopt the standardized ERS/ATS joint statement definitions of severe exacerbations as their primary outcome
 \cite{YanForno2021}
. Additionally, studies should move beyond simple binary (yes/no) metrics to evaluate quantitative temporal traits, such as time-to-first-exacerbation, annual exacerbation frequency, and the temporal distance between consecutive events
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
 \cite{ShiZhang2022}.
3. Resolving the "Missing Heritability" via Rare and Structural Variants
Common single-nucleotide polymorphisms (MAF ≥5\\\\%) evaluated on legacy genotyping arrays account for only a minor fraction of overall heritability
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
:
The Rare Variant Hypothesis: Uncommon (MAF 1−5\\\\%) and rare (MAF <1\\\\%) variants, which are often highly penetrant and population-specific, are poorly represented on standard commercial arrays
 \cite{AslamVijverberg2024}
.
Structural Variations (INDELs): Major genomic structural variations, including copy number variants and insertion-deletion mutations, have been largely ignored in standard GWAS designs
 \cite{ChaChoi2023}.
Future Strategy: The field must transition from array-based genotyping to high-depth next-generation sequencing (NGS), specifically Whole-Exome Sequencing (WES) and Whole-Genome Sequencing (WGS)
 \cite{AslamVijverberg2024}
. Leveraging WGS reference panels (such as TOPMed or CAAPA) will enable precise genotype imputation and fine-mapping of low-frequency coding, non-coding, and regulatory variants
 \cite{DayaRafaels2017}
.
4. Multi-Omic Integration and Tissue-Specific Expression Mapping
A static DNA sequence provides an incomplete view of the dynamic, acute biological events that characterize an asthma flare. A critical gap exists in understanding the functional transcriptomic and epigenetic links between statistical SNPs and downstream tissue pathobiology
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}
 \cite{WangTantisira2016}:
The Tissue-Specific eQTL Gap: The vast majority of expression quantitative trait loci (eQTL) studies are conducted in easily accessible whole blood or peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs), which poorly reflect the disease context
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
 \cite{KimLim2023}. Airway cells exhibit highly tissue-specific, context-dependent transcriptional responses
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
 \cite{PortelliRakkar2021}. For example, the largest bronchial epithelial eQTL databases are based on very small sample sizes
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
 \cite{PortelliRakkar2021}.
Context-Dependent Transcriptional Regulation: Many causal variants may only regulate gene expression under specific cellular conditions (e.g., active viral infection or severe inflammation)
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
 \cite{XuLi2017}.
Future Strategy: Investigators must establish comprehensive eQTL, splicing QTL (sQTL), and expression quantitative trait methylation (eQTM) catalogs specifically within asthma-relevant primary tissues—such as ciliated bronchial epithelial cells (BECs), nasal epithelial cells (NECs), and airway smooth muscle cells (ASMCs)
 \cite{LiChristenson2021}
. This must be paired with multi-omic integration, combining genomics with transcriptomics (RNA-seq), epigenomics (DNA methylation), proteomics, lipidomics, metabolomics, and upper-airway metagenomics (the microbiome) to define molecular endotypes of disease severity
 \cite{AltmanFlynn2021}
.
