5. Standardizing Gene–Environment (GxE) and Epistatic (GxG) Models
Because asthma exacerbations are frequently triggered by exogenous physical insults, genetic variants often remain clinically silent until exposed to environmental stressors
 \cite{FawcettHall2025}
 \cite{ShiZhang2022}. However, several critical gaps remain:
Hypothesis-Driven Exposure Omission: Most historical genetic studies fail to systematically capture and adjust for cumulative environmental exposures, such as viral pathogens (e.g., human rhinovirus C), environmental tobacco smoke (ETS), ambient air pollution (PM 
2.5
​
 , CO, NO 
2
​
 ), and domestic aeroallergens
 \cite{HerreraLuisEspuelaOrtiz2020}
.
Epistasis (Gene–Gene Interaction) Neglect: Complex biological cascades involve multiple genes acting in concert
 \cite{LiYan2019}. Due to the high statistical power requirements, genome-wide searches for epistasis remain exceptionally rare, leading to a major heritability gap
 \cite{WangTantisira2016}
.
Future Strategy: Standardized personal environmental exposure tracking (such as local EPA sensor matching and viral PCR screening) must be integrated into future study designs
 \cite{KelchtermansMarch2024}
 \cite{TseKrajinovic2019}. Advanced systems biology applications, machine learning algorithms (e.g., Random Forest classifiers), and polygenic risk scores (PRSs) must be leveraged to model multi-dimensional gene-environment and epistatic networks
 \cite{HernandezPachecoMeln2022}
.
6. Accounting for Medication Adherence and Clinical Confounders
A major confounding factor in current pharmacogenomic and genetic studies of exacerbations is the failure to adjust for real-world clinical variables, which can bias or completely mask genetic effects
 \cite{SlobRichards2021}
:
Adherence Confounding: While pharmacy prescription databases provide accurate information on dispensed medications, they do not reflect actual patient medication compliance or inhaler technique
 \cite{FarzanVijverberg2018}
. Non-adherence is a potent, independent trigger for exacerbations; if a patient carrying a protective genotype is non-compliant, they may still suffer frequent flares, thereby diluting the apparent genetic effect.
Confounding by Indication: Asthmatics with severe disease are preferentially prescribed higher treatment steps (e.g., combination therapy). Studies must adjust for baseline severity and treatment regimen (using clinical proxies such as the GINA or BTS treatment guidelines) to isolate true pharmacogenomic response variants
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}
.
Future Strategy: Future observational cohorts and clinical trials must incorporate objective measures of medication adherence (e.g., electronic inhaler monitoring) and adjust for baseline lung function, atopy status, smoking history, and comorbidities (such as obesity and gastroesophageal reflux)
 \cite{SlobRichards2021}
.
7. High-Throughput Functional Validation and Clinical Translation
Finally, a massive gap persists between the statistical identification of a disease-associated SNP and its clinical translation into a diagnostic or therapeutic biomarker
 \cite{HernandezPachecoMeln2022}
:
LD Variant Confounding: Over 80\\\\% of identified risk SNPs reside in non-coding or intergenic regions, acting merely as markers in high linkage disequilibrium (LD) with the actual, elusive causal variants
 \cite{NevilleChoi2018}
.
Functional Annotation Gap: The precise biological consequences of the majority of replicated exacerbation-associated genes (including CTNNA3, SEMA3D, CMTR1, and LINC01913) remain completely unknown
 \cite{HerreraLuisEspuelaOrtiz2020}
.
Future Strategy: Studies must transition from candidate-gene designs to genomics-guided target discovery and drug repurposing
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
 \cite{PortelliRakkar2021}. High-throughput functional methodologies—such as capture Hi-C (chromatin conformation capture), multiplexed reporter assays, and CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing—must be deployed in primary human airway cells to validate variant effects on gene transcription, promoter activity, chromatin interactions, and downstream cell signaling
 \cite{VicenteRevez2017}
.
Conclusion
To move the field of asthma genetics beyond the "verge of utopia"
 \cite{HernandezPachecoMeln2022}, future research must transition from unstratified, low-power candidate-gene studies of European populations to prospective, ethnically diverse, phenotypically harmonized multi-omic investigations
 \cite{HerreraLuisOrtega2022}
. Resolving these critical gaps will allow clinical teams to transition from standard reactive guidelines to a genotype-guided, personalized approach to care, enabling the early identification of exacerbation-prone individuals and the development of targeted, curative therapeutic interventions
 \cite{PortelliRakkar2021}
.
