 \cite{AlAhmadAli2025}
. Consequently, single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) within the regulatory/promoter region of TNFA have been extensively evaluated to determine their clinical impact on asthma susceptibility, disease severity, and the frequency of acute severe exacerbations (SAEs).
2. Association of TNF-α rs1800629 (-308G/A) with Asthma Severity and Exacerbations
The rs1800629 polymorphism is a G-to-A transition at position -308 of the TNFA promoter region
 \cite{XuLi2017}
 \cite{PavnRomeroResndizHernndez2017}. The minor A allele (often referred to as the TNF2 allele) has been shown to alter transcription factor binding, resulting in a genetically mediated upregulation of TNFA transcriptional rate and cytokine secretion
 \cite{AlAhmadAli2025}
.
Despite this clear functional mechanism, clinical association studies evaluating its impact on severe or frequent asthma exacerbations have produced highly discordant results, which are primarily categorized below:
A. Studies Demonstrating No Association with Severity or Exacerbation Rates
Several rigorous pediatric and cohort investigations have failed to find a direct single-locus association between rs1800629 and acute severe exacerbations or overall severity:
In an Iranian pediatric cohort evaluated by Razaghian et al. (2023), the homozygous AA genotype of rs1800629 was associated with asthma susceptibility
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}. However, no statistically significant difference was observed in genotype or allele frequencies when stratified by asthma severity (mild, moderate, or severe; P=0.466)
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}
.
Furthermore, there was no correlation between rs1800629 genotypes and the requirement for hospital admission (P=0.659), including intensive care unit (ICU) versus non-ICU hospitalization
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}
.
This lack of correlation in pediatric cohorts is consistent with findings by Bilolikar et al., who reported no statistical association between TNFA rs1800629 and the severity of childhood wheezing or asthma
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}.
In a German pediatric study by Puthothu et al. (2009), rs1800629 showed no significant association with childhood asthma in single-locus analyses (P=0.854), although it contributed to a significant multi-locus risk haplotype (P=0.005)
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}.
B. Studies Supporting an Association with Severity, Exacerbation Risk, or Treatment Failure
Conversely, separate clinical cohorts and meta-analyses suggest that rs1800629 modifies the clinical course, disease control, and vulnerability to severe asthma crises:
Asthmatic Crises and Lack of Control: In adult Caucasians, Li et al. demonstrated that the GA genotype of rs1800629 was significantly associated with clinical phenotypes altering asthma severity, predicting a higher frequency of asthmatic crises and a general lack of symptom control
 \cite{PavnRomeroResndizHernndez2017}.
Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease (AERD): In a Mexican cohort, the GA genotype of rs1800629 was associated with a 2.36-fold increased risk of developing AERD (OR = 2.36, P<0.05), a highly severe, refractory asthma phenotype characterized by profound, acute bronchoconstriction following cyclooxygenase inhibition and rapid structural airway remodeling
 \cite{PavnRomeroResndizHernndez2017}
.
Therapy Non-Response and Failure to Achieve Remission: In patients with severe asthma receiving biologic therapies (such as the anti-IL-4Rα monoclonal antibody dupilumab), genetic variations at rs1800629 significantly modified treatment responses. Specifically, the homozygous wild-type GG genotype was significantly associated with achieving clinical remission (CR), whereas the heterozygous GA genotype was robustly linked to therapy failure
 \cite{AlAhmadAli2025}. This indicates that the A allele acts as a genetic barrier to achieving disease control, predisposing patients to persistent, refractory inflammation.
Replicated Meta-Analyses: Large-scale meta-analyses confirm that the A allele of rs1800629 is significantly associated with elevated asthma risk
 \cite{ZhuZhang2020}
