 \cite{XuLi2017}
 \cite{ZhuZhang2020}. Furthermore, independent cohorts evaluated by Darweeshi et al. and Zedan et al. reported positive correlations between rs1800629 polymorphisms and clinical asthma severity
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}
 \cite{RazaghianParvaneh2023}.
3. Association of TNF-α rs1800630 (-863C/A) with Asthma Severity and Exacerbations
The rs1800630 polymorphism is a cytosine-to-adenine transition located at position -863 within the TNFA promoter region
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}.
Proximity and Haplotype Effects: In genetic mapping studies, the -863C/A SNP is located only six nucleotides apart from the -1037C/T (rs1799724) polymorphism
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}. Because of this extreme physical proximity, these loci are in tight linkage disequilibrium and frequently function as a single haplotype block
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}.
Association with Atopic Asthma: In an evaluation by Sharma et al., rs1800630 (-863C/A) was identified as the most informative promoter SNP, demonstrating a strong correlation with asthma susceptibility, serum IgE levels, and overall TNF-α cytokine production
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}.
Direct Exacerbation Evidence: Although rs1800630 is an established modifier of the pro-inflammatory airway environment, the current candidate-gene and GWAS literature contains limited direct evidence independently associating rs1800630 with longitudinal severe exacerbation rates (e.g., annualized OCS use, ED presentations, or mechanical ventilation)
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}. Its role in exacerbation pathogenesis is primarily hypothesized to occur indirectly through the upregulation of local Th2 inflammation and bronchial hyperresponsiveness rather than via direct pharmacogenomic pathways
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}
 \cite{McGeachieWu2015}.
4. Methodological Considerations and Heterogeneity
The discrepancy in findings across these clinical studies is heavily driven by several confounding and methodological factors:
Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) and Haplotype Block Structures: Because both rs1800629 and rs1800630 are part of the highly complex major histocompatibility complex (MHC) region on chromosome 6p21, simple single-locus analyses frequently fail to capture the true causal variant, which may be better represented by multi-locus TNF haplotypes
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}
.
Gene-Environment Interactions: The clinical penetrance of these promoter variants is highly modulated by environmental co-exposures. For example, the protective effect of the rs1800629 G allele has been shown to be strictly dependent on ambient outdoor ozone levels
 \cite{PuthothuBierbaum2009}.
Ethnic and Geographic Diversity: Differences in minor allele frequencies (MAFs) and background genome architecture across Caucasian, Asian, and Hispanic/Latino populations can alter or entirely mask the statistical effects of these candidate loci
