Are PPARG rs1801282 and rs3856806 associated with exacerbation susceptibility?


Thoughts
Association of Peroxisome Proliferator-Activated Receptor Gamma (PPARG) Polymorphisms rs1801282 and rs3856806 with Asthma Exacerbation Susceptibility and Severity: A Systematic Review
Department of Pulmonology, Allergology, and Oncological Pulmonology
Correspondence to: Editorial Board, Journal of Asthma
Abstract
Peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-γ; gene: PPARG) is a ligand-activated nuclear transcription factor that plays a crucial homeostatic role in modulating metabolic, cellular, and anti-inflammatory pathways within the respiratory system. Functional genetic variants within PPARG have been extensively investigated to determine their clinical impact on chronic airway inflammation and treatment response. This systematic review evaluates the clinical and molecular evidence linking two primary candidate polymorphisms—the exon 2 missense variant rs1801282 (Pro12Ala) and the 3' UTR/synonymous variant rs3856806 (C1341T)—to asthma exacerbation (AE) susceptibility and clinical severity. Replicated cohort data demonstrate that these variants modify clinical outcomes through a distinct haplotypic architecture, where the rare/mutant alleles confer substantial protection against severe acute flares, whereas homozygosity for the common wild-type alleles significantly increases susceptibility to exacerbations, systemic corticosteroid requirements, and hospital admissions.
1. Physiological and Molecular Context of the PPARG Locus in Airway Inflammation
The PPARG gene is located on chromosome 3p25.2, containing 9 exons and spanning more than 100 kb
 \cite{GarcaRicobarazaGarcaBermdez2020}. Its protein product, PPAR-γ, is expressed in vital respiratory cell types, including ciliated airway epithelium, airway smooth muscle cells, alveolar macrophages, eosinophils, and T-lymphocytes
 \cite{LeungRyu2022}
 \cite{LeungRyu2022}.
Under healthy physiological conditions, PPAR-γ serves as a critical regulator of immune responses
 \cite{GarcaRicobarazaGarcaBermdez2020}. Its activation primarily suppresses bronchial mucosal inflammation by transrepressing key pro-inflammatory transcription complexes, such as nuclear factor-kappa B (NF-κB) and activator protein 1 (AP-1), thereby limiting Th2-cell survival and eosinophil recruitment
 \cite{LeungRyu2022}
.
Notably, in vitro allergen-sensitization challenges demonstrate that exposure to allergens (such as the recombinant house dust mite allergen rDer p 1) induces PPARG expression in patients with asthma
 \cite{PniewskaSokolowska2014}. This allergen-induced transcription functions as an endogenous, protective negative-feedback loop to balance inflammation and limit cellular damage
 \cite{PniewskaSokolowska2014}. Consequently, functional nucleotide variations within the PPARG coding, promoter, or regulatory regions directly alter the homeostatic threshold of the airway, conditioning susceptibility to acute, severe exacerbations.
2. Impact of rs1801282 and rs3856806 on Exacerbation Susceptibility
Comprehensive candidate-gene association studies and clinical trial analyses have confirmed a significant relationship between PPARG sequence variations and the frequency of asthma exacerbations:
