Do TGFB1 rs1800469 and rs2241712 contribute to exacerbation risk through airway remodeling pathways?


Thoughts
The Role of TGFB1 promoter Polymorphisms rs1800469 and rs2241712 in Airway Remodeling and Asthma Exacerbation Susceptibility: A Systematic Review
Department of Basic Medical Sciences and Pulmonology
Correspondence to: Editorial Board, Journal of Asthma
Abstract
Bronchial asthma is a highly heterogeneous chronic inflammatory disease of the respiratory tract characterized by bronchial hyperresponsiveness, reversible airflow obstruction, and chronic airway wall structural changes collectively termed airway remodeling
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2025}. Transforming growth factor-beta 1 (TGF-β1) is a pleiotropic cytokine consistently identified as a master regulator of this remodeling cascade, stimulating subepithelial fibrosis, extracellular matrix (ECM) deposition, and myofibroblast differentiation
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
. Single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) within the TGFB1 regulatory regions have been extensively investigated to determine their impact on gene expression, structural airway changes, and the subsequent heritability of severe asthma exacerbations (AEs).
This systematic review synthesizes the molecular and clinical evidence regarding two prominent regulatory SNPs—rs1800469 (-509C/T) and rs2241712 (C/T)—evaluating their functional contribution to asthma severity and exacerbation risk through established airway remodeling pathways.
1. Pathophysiological Role of TGF-β1 in Airway Remodeling and Hyperresponsiveness
In the chronic inflammatory microenvironment of the asthmatic airway, ciliated bronchial epithelial cells, invading inflammatory cells (primarily activated eosinophils), and lung fibroblasts secrete elevated levels of active TGF-β1
 \cite{KraikTota2024}
. The canonical signal transduction of TGF-β1 involves binding to the transmembrane serine-threonine kinase receptors TβR-II and TβR-I (ALK5), which subsequently phosphorylate downstream Smad2 and Smad3 transcription factors
 \cite{KraikTota2024}
. This Smad complex translocates to the nucleus to coordinate the transcription of over 500 genes involved in structural tissue changes
 \cite{PanekKarbownik2022}
 \cite{DerynckZhang2003}. Additionally, TGF-β1 initiates non-canonical, Smad-independent signaling via mitogen-activated protein kinases (MAPK/ERK), c-Jun N-terminal kinase (JNK), and p38 pathways
 \cite{KraikTota2024}
.
These integrated signaling cascades drive the classic features of airway remodeling through several key biological mechanisms:
Extracellular Matrix (ECM) Synthesis: TGF-β1 stimulates the chemoattraction and proliferation of structural fibroblasts, inducing their transition into highly active myofibroblasts (fibroblast-to-myofibroblast transition, or FMT)
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2025}
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}. This transition causes excessive deposition of ECM proteins, including collagens type I and III, fibronectin, tenascin, and proteoglycans in the subepithelial space, leading to reticular basement membrane thickening
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
.
Inhibition of Matrix Degradation: Concurrently, TGF-β1 downregulates matrix-depleting proteins by inhibiting the transcriptional expression of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) and collagenases, which limits the physical degradation of newly synthesized ECM
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
.
Airway Smooth Muscle (ASM) Dysfunction: TGF-β1 promotes the proliferation and hypertrophy of bronchial myocytes and directly enhances ASM excitation-contraction coupling
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}
. This chronic physical remodeling alters the baseline elasticity of the bronchial wall, causing increased airway stiffness and hyperresponsiveness (AHR) that leads to persistent, irreversible airflow obstruction
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
.
2. Functional Impact of the promoter Polymorphism rs1800469 (-509C/T)
The single-nucleotide polymorphism rs1800469 represents a base substitution (frequently characterized as a C-to-T transition at position -509) within the proximal promoter region of the TGFB1 gene
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
.
A. Molecular Alteration of Transcriptional Activity
Because rs1800469 is located in the regulatory promoter, it does not alter the primary amino acid sequence of the synthesized TGF-β1 protein; instead, it modifies the rate of gene transcription
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}.
Transcription Factor Binding: Biophysical assays demonstrate that the T allele prevents the transcriptional repressor AP1 from binding to the promoter, while simultaneously altering the interaction of this region with the transcription factor YY1
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}
 \cite{PlichtaPanek2025}.
Upregulated Protein Production: This loss of AP1-mediated silencing leads to a significant increase in the transcriptional activity of the TGFB1 gene, directly translating to elevated plasma and serum concentrations of TGF-β1 in carriers of the T allele
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
.
