The rs2241712 variant is another highly evaluated promoter polymorphism in the TGFB1 gene
 \cite{KowinJonakowski2024}
. Evidence from prospective, longitudinal birth cohorts demonstrates that rs2241712 does not act as an isolated susceptibility marker, but rather functions as a critical gene-environment (GxE) modifier of severe exacerbations:
The Sharma et al. Paradigm: In a landmark study evaluating two distinct pediatric cohorts—including Costa Rican children and subjects from the Childhood Asthma Management Program (CAMP) trial—investigators analyzed the interaction between TGFB1 genotypes and domestic aeroallergen exposure
 \cite{ParkTantisira2017}
.
Aeroallergen Interaction: The researchers demonstrated that domestic house dust mite (HDM) exposure (Der p 1 levels ≥10 μg/g of dust) significantly modified the effect of the rs2241712 polymorphism on severe asthma exacerbations
 \cite{ParkTantisira2017}
.
Exacerbation Outcomes: Specifically, children carrying the high-risk genotype who were exposed to high domestic dust mite allergen loads experienced a significantly elevated frequency of severe, acute exacerbations requiring emergency department visits or hospitalizations
 \cite{ParkTantisira2017}
. Under low allergen exposure conditions (<10 μg/g), this genetic risk was not expressed, highlighting a strict GxE interaction requirement
 \cite{PalmoCantarelli2021}.
